==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|de}} {{term|Hinterland|lang=de}}, from {{term|hinter||behind|lang=de}} + {{term|Land||land|lang=de}}. First used in English in 1888 by {{w|George Chisholm}} in his work ''Handbook of Commercial Geography''.<ref>[http://www.britannica.com/eb/article-9040530/hinterland Definition of the term "hinterland" on Encyclopædia Britannica]</ref> The term is characteristic of [[thalassocratic]] analysis of space.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɪntə(ɹ)ˌlænd/}} {{SAMPA|/"hInt@(r)%l{nd/}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# The [[land]] immediately next to, and [[inland]] from, a [[coast]].
# The [[rural]] [[territory]] surrounding an [[urban]] area, especially a [[port]].
# A [[remote]] or [[undeveloped]] area, a [[backwater]].
# {{figurative}} Anything [[vague]] or [[ill-defined]], ''especially'' one that is ill understood.
#* '''2007''', Lesley Jeffries, ''[http://www.palgrave.com/products/title.aspx?PID=262132 Textual Construction of the Female Body]'', abstract
#*: This approach utilizes concepts such as naming, describing, contrasting and equating to access the '''hinterland''' between structure and meaning, and to map out the subtle ways in which texts can naturalise the ideology of the perfect female form.

====Synonyms====
* See: [[Wikisaurus:remote place]]
* (the) [[sticks]]

